00:00:07.619 --> 00:00:10.770 align:start position:0%
big birds such as geese cranes and  ibises tend to fly in V formations the  aerodynamics of the flight patterns have  fascinated biologists and aircraft  designers for decades to take a more  detailed look at the birds flight  scientists studied Northern bald ibises  from zoo Vienna these birds aren't going  to be on exhibit but are to be released  into the wild to teach the birds a  migration route to Italy volunteers  wrote in the back of a microlight plane  and shouted for the youngsters to follow  the birds were equipment that recorded  their exact position and every wing beat  for 43 minutes the recording equipment  showed that the birds can time their  wing beats so their wingtips either  match the path taken by the wing  movement of the bird in front of them or  is deliberately out of phase with it  whether the bird's wings were in sync or  out of sync dependent on their alignment  with the birds in front of them when  flying diagonally behind another bird in  the V formation the birds tended to  match the path of the front fliers  linked it as a result the trailing bird  gains lick from the one in front of it  but when one bird was directly behind  another one flying with wings out of  phase was best the position reduces the  large downwash of air pushed down by the  front flier the results were published  january fifteenth in nature they are the  first to record the complex wing  flapping coordination pattern of free  flying birds
 

